{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2255 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "fa8e7e73b76785192c73361cf90f6363d7c1ca6d7955aeb7acd5da14f9babec1" :edb/db-before-t 2254 :edb/db-after-t 2255 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535567 50 #inst "2026-10-09T02:41:29.242Z" 13194139535567 true] [17592186068696 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355440 13194139535567 true] [17592186083213 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355443 13194139535567 true] [17592186087592 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355432 13194139535567 true] [17592186092213 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355447 13194139535567 true] [17592186092320 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355450 13194139535567 true] [17592186100935 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355453 13194139535567 true] [17592186110533 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355435 13194139535567 true] [17592186355432 1017 #edb/ref 17592186355433 13194139535567 true] [17592186355432 1089 "q-167932" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355432 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535567 true] [17592186355432 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/assets/ae44c9f19c7afc26c399999105b7d17c6e2e6b3e020e7b419065a8514741dc9b.png)\n\n \n\nConsider a circle with radius $3$ centered at the point $(3,0),$ shown above.\n\nCurrent part:\n\nWrite the equation of this circle in the form $(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355432 1113 #uuid "8aa5ec7e-35c7-5308-9c01-0b177011d114" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355432 1145 "The equation of a circle with radius $r$  centered at the point $(a,b)$ in rectangular coordinates in standard form is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the circle is centered at $(3,0)$ and the radius of the circle is $r=3$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the circle in rectangular coordinates is \n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.\n$$" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355433 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355434 13194139535567 true] [17592186355433 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535567 true] [17592186355433 1041 "field-1" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355433 1104 #edb/ref 17592186355434 13194139535567 true] [17592186355433 1110 #uuid "daa87df0-384d-5845-b3ae-a2f670937ca2" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355434 1034 "(x-3)^2+(y-0)^2=9" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355434 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535567 true] [17592186355434 1111 #uuid "ccfafb5a-9d68-5430-9678-7f8ae2289013" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355435 1017 #edb/ref 17592186355436 13194139535567 true] [17592186355435 1017 #edb/ref 17592186355438 13194139535567 true] [17592186355435 1089 "q-167933" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355435 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535567 true] [17592186355435 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/assets/ae44c9f19c7afc26c399999105b7d17c6e2e6b3e020e7b419065a8514741dc9b.png)\n\n \n\nConsider a circle with radius $3$ centered at the point $(3,0),$ shown above.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nWrite the equation of this circle in the form $(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $(x-3)^2+(y-0)^2=9$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nWrite the equations of the upper and lower semicircles.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Upper: $\\,\\,\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Lower: $\\,\\,\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355435 1113 #uuid "cb21d9e6-1c6c-57bb-a085-f049842e2ff8" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355435 1145 "We make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =9-(x-3)^{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{{y}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^{2}} \\\\ |y| & =\\sqrt{9-({x}^{2}-6x+9)} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n* the equation with the positive square root, $y=\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}$, describes the upper semicircle, and \n\n* the equation with the negative square root, $y=-\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}$, describes the lower semicircle." 13194139535567 true] [17592186355436 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355437 13194139535567 true] [17592186355436 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535567 true] [17592186355436 1041 "field-1" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355436 1104 #edb/ref 17592186355437 13194139535567 true] [17592186355436 1110 #uuid "77cd618d-acc5-5bad-b798-3f9c28b43a83" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355437 1034 "\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355437 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535567 true] [17592186355437 1111 #uuid "8cdc1ae0-9e9c-595a-9116-7f50c4059e59" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355438 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355439 13194139535567 true] [17592186355438 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535567 true] [17592186355438 1041 "field-2" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355438 1104 #edb/ref 17592186355439 13194139535567 true] [17592186355438 1110 #uuid "f70ec80e-aee3-5a66-8875-7256c144673a" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355439 1034 "-\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355439 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535567 true] [17592186355439 1111 #uuid "40ef8d58-b9fb-5237-9e30-b23320c77477" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355440 1017 #edb/ref 17592186355441 13194139535567 true] [17592186355440 1089 "q-167934" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355440 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535567 true] [17592186355440 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/assets/ae44c9f19c7afc26c399999105b7d17c6e2e6b3e020e7b419065a8514741dc9b.png)\n\n \n\nConsider a circle with radius $3$ centered at the point $(3,0),$ shown above.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nWrite the equation of this circle in the form $(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $(x-3)^2+(y-0)^2=9$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nWrite the equations of the upper and lower semicircles.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Upper: $\\,\\,\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Lower: $\\,\\,\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nConstruct an integral that represents the area of this circle using rectangular coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{0}^{6}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}x$" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355440 1113 #uuid "efa63c4c-aff1-5662-a025-40591a1f5ee0" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355440 1145 "Since  $y=\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}$ represents the upper semicircle  and  $y=-\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}$ represents the lower semicircle, the area $A$ of the circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}[(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}[(\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}})-(-\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}})]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}2\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355441 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355442 13194139535567 true] [17592186355441 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535567 true] [17592186355441 1041 "field-1" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355441 1104 #edb/ref 17592186355442 13194139535567 true] [17592186355441 1110 #uuid "64f4e816-a967-506c-9f77-f8a829a4caa4" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355442 1034 "2\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355442 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535567 true] [17592186355442 1111 #uuid "dc964dfa-8698-5dd3-baf1-b3b908ce2269" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355443 1017 #edb/ref 17592186355444 13194139535567 true] [17592186355443 1089 "q-167935" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355443 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535567 true] [17592186355443 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/assets/ae44c9f19c7afc26c399999105b7d17c6e2e6b3e020e7b419065a8514741dc9b.png)\n\n \n\nConsider a circle with radius $3$ centered at the point $(3,0),$ shown above.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nWrite the equation of this circle in the form $(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $(x-3)^2+(y-0)^2=9$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nWrite the equations of the upper and lower semicircles.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Upper: $\\,\\,\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Lower: $\\,\\,\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nConstruct an integral that represents the area of this circle using rectangular coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{0}^{6}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nConstruct an integral that represents the arc length of this circle in rectangular coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{0}^{6}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}x$" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355443 1113 #uuid "0d48b736-e08a-5fcb-89ef-4baef85fd7d9" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355443 1145 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, ${f}_{1}(x)=\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}$ represents the upper semicircle and ${f}_{2}(x)=-\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}$ represents the lower semicircle with $a=0$ and $b=6.$ \n\nFirst, we find the derivative of ${f}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3-x}{\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}_{1}^{′}(x)$ into the arc length formula, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}_{1} & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\sqrt{1+[{f}_{1}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{(3-x)^{2}}{6x-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\sqrt{\\frac{6x-{x}^{2}}{6x-{x}^{2}}+\\frac{9-6x+{x}^{2}}{6x-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the same manner, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}_{2} & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L={L}_{1}+{L}_{2} & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355444 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355445 13194139535567 true] [17592186355444 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355446 13194139535567 true] [17592186355444 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535567 true] [17592186355444 1041 "field-1" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355444 1104 #edb/ref 17592186355445 13194139535567 true] [17592186355444 1110 #uuid "1caacf60-e80b-5931-8f71-8e28b6c628ca" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355445 1034 "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{6x-x^2}}" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355445 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535567 true] [17592186355445 1111 #uuid "8babcffe-a60c-56b8-aa30-e9e2322a9060" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355446 1034 "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}}" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355446 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535567 true] [17592186355446 1111 #uuid "073eee2b-5061-55c2-a299-9964960b5045" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355447 1017 #edb/ref 17592186355448 13194139535567 true] [17592186355447 1089 "q-167937" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355447 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535567 true] [17592186355447 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/assets/ae44c9f19c7afc26c399999105b7d17c6e2e6b3e020e7b419065a8514741dc9b.png)\n\n \n\nConsider a circle with radius $3$ centered at the point $(3,0),$ shown above.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nWrite the equation of this circle in the form $(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $(x-3)^2+(y-0)^2=9$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nWrite the equations of the upper and lower semicircles.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Upper: $\\,\\,\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Lower: $\\,\\,\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nConstruct an integral that represents the area of this circle using rectangular coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{0}^{6}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nConstruct an integral that represents the arc length of this circle in rectangular coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{0}^{6}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{6x-x^2}}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nGiven that the polar equation of the circle is $r=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ construct an integral that represents the area of this circle in polar coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta $" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355447 1113 #uuid "2bb3d235-8608-5dc5-b1e6-8b31b43eeb0e" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355447 1145 "In general, the area of the region bounded by a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ and the rays $\\theta ={\\theta }_{1}$ and $\\theta ={\\theta }_{2}$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of our circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}36{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}18{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355448 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355449 13194139535567 true] [17592186355448 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535567 true] [17592186355448 1041 "field-1" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355448 1104 #edb/ref 17592186355449 13194139535567 true] [17592186355448 1110 #uuid "bb100120-e6fc-5d5f-97dd-2d01d6c34520" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355449 1034 "18(\\cos(\\theta))^2" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355449 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535567 true] [17592186355449 1111 #uuid "97479b02-9217-5d9c-bda1-7f4b0ab277bd" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355450 1017 #edb/ref 17592186355451 13194139535567 true] [17592186355450 1089 "q-167938" 13194139535567 true] [17592186355450 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535567 true] [17592186355450 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/assets/ae44c9f19c7afc26c399999105b7d17c6e2e6b3e020e7b419065a8514741dc9b.png)\n\n \n\nConsider a circle with radius $3$ centered at the point $(3,0),$ shown above.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nWrite the equation of this circle in the form $(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                  